From 8a4f3a4abacf516843a20dd0e1da13c3e8786cee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 05:07:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] feat(control-plane): add a controller manager that drives
 the reconciler

Nothing ran ReconciliationController outside of tests, so resources
written through the API were never reconciled. Add a controller package:

- WorkQueue: a keyed delay queue with client-go semantics. A key is
  processed by at most one worker at a time, delayed adds keep the
  earliest due time, and failures back off exponentially with jitter.
- ControllerManager: records the current change sequence, lists all
  managed resources to rebuild the queue, then polls resource_changes
  incrementally (pruning what it has read) with a periodic full resync
  as a safety net. Status changes, including its own writes, do not
  trigger a reconcile.
- Status is written with a CAS on the version read before reconciling.
  On a conflict the resource is re-read; the write is retried only if
  uid, generation and operation (reconcile/terminate) are unchanged,
  otherwise the result is dropped and the resource is requeued.
- Dedup keys are recorded by the manager only after the status write
  succeeds, so a crash in between causes a retry instead of a resource
  whose outcome was never persisted. The reconciler must therefore be
  built with record_dedup_keys=False.
- requeue_after is honoured; None means wait for the next change or
  resync. stop() lets in-flight reconciles finish before cancelling.

The controller is a single writer (ADR-013): it runs inside the API
process and must not run in several processes or replicas at once.
